+## Training budget and warmup interpretation
+
+The real initial log shows all eight workers `Total steps: 6, num_warmup_steps: 1`. Independent source review confirms ray_trainer overwrites actor optimizer total steps from the trainer budget; fsdp_workers computes `int(0.285 * total_steps)`, builds constant LambdaLR warmup, and steps the scheduler once after an outer update with positive optimizer calls. Therefore `actor/lr` is the scheduler value after that outer, applicable to the next outer; it is not the LR used for all micro/mini updates just finished.
+
+A CPU probe executing the actual scheduler function gives acceptance budget6 applied outer LR multipliers `[0,1,1,1,1]`, while formal budget501/warmup142 gives `[0,1/142,2/142,3/142,4/142]` over the first five outers. Probe baseLR1e-6 is only a normalized illustration, not an assertion of the real run baseLR. Both schedules advance per outer rather than per each of the ten optimizer calls. Thus the approved five-outer short-budget acceptance has a different LR trajectory from the first five iterations of the formal 500-outer run. No protocol change or restart is required here, but final metric differences must be described as short-run acceptance/pilot outcomes, not formal first-five effects or final superiority. Evidence `evidence/budget_warmup_schedule_independent_probe.json`; actual eight-worker records are in `job2875_training_initial_full.txt`. First outer uses LR0, so its optimizer calls alone cannot prove weight movement. Final changed tensor evidence remains required.
